## `for mut x in list` (own.excl.5). A struct element is a pointer to its
## fields, so a write through `x` changes the list's element in place and
## the loop lowers as a plain `for`. A scalar or `str` element is a value:
## the variable's assignments also set the list's element (`assign_param`),
## so every way out of the loop leaves the list changed.
use ast

## Whether `for mut` over elements of type `e` lowers; a problem after not.
fn Lower.for_mut_ok(mut self, e Ty, pos ast.Pos) bool
    match e
        Struct(_) => true
        # A list or table element is a pointer too: writes through it land in place.
        List(_) | Map(_, _) | Set(_) => true
        _ =>
            if whole_elem(e)
                return true
            problem(pos, "`for mut` isn't in the native subset yet")
            false

## Whether a `for mut` variable of type `e` changes only by a whole assignment
## (`x = v`), which sets the list's element: a scalar, `str`, `bytes`, an enum,
## an optional or a tuple (ADR 1109).
fn whole_elem(e Ty) bool
    if e.opt_inner() != none
        return true
    match e
        Int | Bool | Char | Float | Str | Bytes | Sized(_) | Enum(_) | Tuple(_) => true
        _ => false

## Note that `var`, declared as the element at `index` of `list`, writes through.
fn Lower.note_elem(mut self, var str, elem Ty, list int, index int, pos ast.Pos)
    if whole_elem(elem)
        key := lookup(var) else return
        facts.elem_ref["{fname}|{key}"] = ElemRef(list: list, index: index, pos: pos)
